import Mathlib

/-!
# TI = TI_Sp with only Mathlib as a dependency

## The published open question

Chen, Grochow, Qiao, Tang, and Zhang explicitly asked whether TI_Sp equals TI
in Open Question 8 of their ITCS 2024 paper, Section 1.5, page 31:10.
The full version numbers this Open Question 1.8 and also asks about the
orthogonal and unitary cases. The question addressed here is its symplectic case:
ordinary three-tensor isomorphism versus three independent symplectic actions.

## The previously known half

Their Theorem 4 (Theorem 1.4 in the full version) proves the reduction

  Sp3TI -> GL3TI, hence TI_Sp contained in TI.

The construction encodes preservation of the bilinear forms as a tensor system,
then compiles that system to ordinary tensor isomorphism using the tensor-system
universality theorem of Futorny, Grochow, and Sergeichuk [FGS19, Theorem 1.1].
This containment is prior work and is credited to those sources.

## How our proof supplies the missing half

Our construction proves GL3TI -> Sp3TI by hyperbolic zero-padding. Replace each
factor U by H(U) = U plus its dual, equipped with the alternating pairing
omega((u, a), (v, b)) = b(u) - a(v), and place the original tensor in the
all-primal block. Every side length doubles, and every output coefficient is
an original coefficient or zero.

A GL transporter P lifts to the symplectic transporter P plus P^(-transpose).
That classical lift proves preservation of isomorphism. The decisive additional
step is reflection of isomorphism under arbitrary symplectic transporters:
intrinsic mode supports transform covariantly, so a transporter of padded
tensors restricts to isomorphisms of their active primal supports. Extending
these maps to the original ambient factors recovers a GL transporter.
This argument handles nonconcise tensors and every field, including characteristic 2.

Thus our padding proves the missing TI contained in TI_Sp direction. Combined
with the known opposite direction, the reductions give equality. The contribution
is the orbit-reflecting padding and its support-recovery proof; the hyperbolic
GL embedding and the previously known containment retain their original attribution.

## Where the two halves are proved in this file

All names below are under the namespace `TISpSingleFile`.

* `SymplecticTI.gl_iff_padded`: the hyperbolic orbit-equivalence theorem.
* `Meta.PaddingProjection20260902.padProjection`: the concrete GL3TI -> Sp3TI projection.
* `Meta.FGSEncodingIso20260902.sp3tiProjectionClosed`: the concrete Sp3TI -> GL3TI
  projection, including the tensor-system gadget and partition-deletion proofs.
* `ti_eq_tisp`: equality of the classes generated by these two problems under
  the polynomial-size coordinate projections defined in this file.

The known half is proved internally here as well, so the final Lean theorem
has no cited reduction axiom or assumed gadget. The formal scope remains
projection-class equality: this file does not formalize uniform Turing-machine
running-time bounds. The published question uses polynomial-time reductions;
that computational interpretation must retain the cited field/model conventions.

## References

[CGQTZ24] Zhili Chen, Joshua A. Grochow, Youming Qiao, Gang Tang, and Chuanqi Zhang.
On the Complexity of Isomorphism Problems for Tensors, Groups, and Polynomials III:
Actions by Classical Groups. ITCS 2024, LIPIcs 287, article 31, pages 31:1-31:23.
Theorem 4, page 31:6; Open Question 8, page 31:10.
https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITCS.2024.31
Full version: https://arxiv.org/html/2306.03135v2
(Theorem 1.4; Section 4; Open Question 1.8 in Section 1.5.)

[FGS19] Vyacheslav Futorny, Joshua A. Grochow, and Vladimir V. Sergeichuk.
Wildness for tensors. Linear Algebra and its Applications 566 (2019), 212-244.
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.laa.2018.12.022

## Standalone formalization

This standalone file contains the complete local dependency closure of the closed
three-tensor equality certificate, including hyperbolic padding, support cancellation,
the reverse FGS encoding, partition deletion, and polynomial output-size bounds.

The final theorem is `TISpSingleFile.ti_eq_tisp`. Its reduction notion is the
polynomial-size coordinate projection structure defined below. It does not assert
